Let me analyze this PHP code carefully.

```php
trait Counter
{
    public static int $n = 0;
}
class CX { use Counter; }
class CY { use Counter; }
CX::$n = 1;
echo CY::$n;
```

In PHP, when a trait is used by multiple classes, each class that uses the trait gets its own copy of the static properties. Static properties in traits are not shared between classes that use the trait - each class has its own instance of the static property.

So when `CX::$n = 1;` is set, it only affects CX's copy of `$n`. CY's copy remains at its initial value of `0`.

Therefore, `echo CY::$n;` will output `0`.

